#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:134
msgid "Tigers and goats"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:135
msgid "Four tigers against twenty goats. The tigers start on the corners; the goats start in hand, off the board. The goats move first."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:140
msgid "Placing the goats"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:141
msgid "While goats are left in hand, every goat turn is a placement: one goat onto any empty point. Goats on the board can't move until all twenty are placed. The tigers move in between."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:145
msgid "How a tiger moves"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:146
msgid "A tiger steps along a line to the next empty point. Diagonals run only through every other point, so some points have eight neighbours and the rest four or fewer."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:153 titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:230 titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:299
msgid "Capturing"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:154
msgid "A tiger captures by jumping over a goat next to it on a line, onto the empty point just beyond. The goat leaves the board. One goat per jump, and capturing is never compulsory."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:160
msgid "Safety in numbers"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:161
msgid "A goat with another goat right behind it, or the edge of the board, can't be jumped: the tiger needs an empty point to land on. Goats that stay together are safe."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:166
msgid "Moving the goats"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:167
msgid "Once all the goats are in play, a goat steps along a line to the next empty point, just as a tiger does. Goats never jump and never capture."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:177 titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:186
msgid "How the tigers win"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:178
msgid "In Parlett's version the tigers must capture all twenty goats. Everything else is as in the standard game."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:187
msgid "The tigers win by capturing five goats."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:195
msgid "How the goats win"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:196
msgid "The goats win when no tiger can move: every tiger is hemmed in by goats it can't jump."
msgstr ""

#. Walkthrough step title: the rules for a drawn game.
#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:204 titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:266 titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:325
msgid "Draws"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:205
msgid "Once all the goats are placed, no move may bring back a position that has already occurred. The game is a draw if the tigers go 50 moves without capturing, or if every move left would repeat a position."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:218
msgid "The jaguar and the dogs"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:219
msgid "One jaguar against fourteen dogs. The jaguar starts in the middle of the board and the dogs fill the far half. The jaguar moves first."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:224
msgid "Moving"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:225
msgid "Each turn, one piece steps along a line to the next empty point, in any direction. The triangle is part of the board, and either side may go in."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:231
msgid "The jaguar captures by jumping over a dog next to it on a line, onto the empty point just beyond. The dog leaves the board. Capturing is optional."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:237
msgid "Chains of captures"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:238
msgid "After a capture the jaguar may jump again, and again, changing direction as it likes, and it may stop after any jump. No dog can be jumped twice in one turn."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:244
msgid "Strength in pairs"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:245
msgid "Two dogs in a row along a line protect each other: the jaguar has nowhere to land."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:250
msgid "How the jaguar wins"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:251
msgid "The jaguar wins by capturing five dogs."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:258 titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:306
msgid "How the dogs win"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:259
msgid "The dogs win when the jaguar can't move at all."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:267
msgid "If the same position comes up three times, or the jaguar goes 50 moves without capturing, the game is a draw."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:278
msgid "The puma and the dogs"
msgstr ""

#. "The tower" is the name of Komikan's triangle in the Spanish sources ("la torre"). Use the ordinary word for tower.
#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:280
msgid "Komikan is a Mapuche hunting game. The puma starts inside the tower, the triangle, and twelve dogs wait at the far end. The puma moves first."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:285
msgid "The puma"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:286
msgid "The puma steps along any line, in any direction, forward or back."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:291
msgid "Moving the dogs"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:292
msgid "A dog steps the same way: along any line to the next empty point, forward, sideways or back. Only the puma can jump."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:300
msgid "The puma captures by jumping over a lone dog onto the empty point beyond. It may jump again and again, changing direction, and stop after any jump. Two dogs in a row protect each other."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:307
msgid "The dogs win by hemming the puma in so that it can't move."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:315
msgid "How the puma wins"
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:316
msgid "The puma wins as soon as it has eaten three dogs."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:326
msgid "If the same position comes up three times, or the puma goes 50 moves without capturing, the game is a draw."
msgstr ""

#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:330
msgid "A reconstruction"
msgstr ""

#. Trapial-Kuzen is the name of a modern Mapuche version of Komikan (Argentina); keep it as is.
#: titles/common/hunt_tutorials.gd:332
msgid "In the accounts written down a century ago, the dogs may only go forward, and then a puma that stays in the tower can never be caught. Here the dogs move and the puma wins as in Trapial-Kuzen, a modern Mapuche version of the game. The tower is ordinary board, and the puma moves first unless you change it in the settings."
msgstr ""

#. iPhone/iPad system prompt, under Apple's question "Allow Bagh Chal to track your activity across other companies' apps and websites?". Says why: more relevant ads, and ads keep the game free.
#: titles/common/title_config.gd:202
msgid "This lets us show you ads that are more relevant. Bagh Chal is free because of ads."
msgstr ""

#. iPhone/iPad system prompt, under Apple's question "Bagh Chal would like to find and connect to devices on your local network". Says why: to play someone nearby on the same Wi-Fi.
#: titles/common/title_config.gd:204
msgid "Bagh Chal looks for games on your Wi-Fi network so you can play someone nearby."
msgstr ""

